Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public.
Dispute 21 - Day 19

Are "Verified" Results a BIG Joke! 
Round 2...  

Bruce Once you receive your responses from Round 1, which should
take somewhere between 30-45 days, you should have some accounts if not
a good portion of your unverified accounts removed. If you receive a
response stating that the account is yours or has been verified, no
worries, you now have more leverage than ever. If you received a
response other than verified, go back to the previous lesson to
determine your next step. Now, let's go a little bit more in-depth
about what "verified" means, how they conclude such foolishness and how
you can use the very things they've put in place to fight against you
to make them fold. 

**Tip:**You don't have to wait 30-45 days for a response. You can get 

**AGGRESSIVE**. If you left a review and have received my Personal
Information Attack Pack (once you've followed that guide to access
your online real-time reports), just wait 3-4 weeks after mailing off
your Round 1 letters and use you Equifax Confirmation Number and your
Experian Report Number to check your credit report and see what items
have been removed. You can start drafting up your letters and sending
them off for the 2nd round of disputes even before you've received
your consumer disclosures in the mail.

Bruce, Does An Actual Investigation Ever Take Place???

The dispute system was created to provide the consumer recourse when
there is an inaccuracy on their credit report. The way the system has
turned into an automated dispute process is fundamentally flawed. There
are no real investigations, but rather an automated process of sending
and receiving information between the financial institution and the
credit reporting agencies. The process barely satisfies the FCRA
regulations and frequently violates the law.

The investigations usually involve the furnisher (or creditor) comparing
the information in their computers to what is reported in your credit
file. Typically the bureau accepts what the furnishing company tells
them. This is referred to as "parroting," as the reporting agency is
simply repeating what the furnisher has said. There is no additional
investigation to review records or contact the consumer.

The automated dispute system is designed to convert the detailed
reporting information into a simple three-digit code and a couple of
lines of narrative. There is a standard form which all three reporting
agencies use for this process called the Consumer Dispute Verification
form (CDV). If the form is submitted electronically, it is known as an
Automated Consumer Dispute Verification (ACDV) form. The ACDV form
consists of a few pieces of information: the consumer's identifying
information, the codes which summarize the dispute, and sometimes a
couple of lines of narrative.

What the WHA!!!! Mad Yet??? Well just keep reading...

The credit bureaus' e-OSCAR system (Online Solution for Complete and
Accurate Reporting) offers 26 available dispute codes: not his/hers,
account closed, account paid as agreed, etc. These codes are what drive
the system, yet they are inadequate in many instances to convey a
complete picture of the actual dispute.

Out of the 26 codes, the credit bureaus actually assign almost 85% of
the disputes to only five of the 26 available codes.

CODE PERCENTAGE

Not his/hers                                       
                                          30.5%
Disputes present/previous account status/history               
        21.2%
Claims inaccurate information. Did not provide specific
dispute  16.8%Disputes amounts                         
                                               
8.8%
Claims account closed by consumer                         
       

**            7.0%**

TOTAL           84.3%

So Bruce, if your dispute reason states "this account is
inaccurate" and you provide nothing else, the 2-3 digit reason code
assigned to your dispute will likely be "Claims inaccurate information.
Did not provide specific dispute." This code triggers the response "Not
Specific" that your client receives back in the consumer disclosure.
This is why it's important to make sure your dispute reasons are written
clearly using the 3 R Formula and similar language discussed in the
previous lesson. 

If your dispute reason states "the date of last activity is inaccurate"
your dispute is likely to get coded "Not his/hers." This code triggers
the response "We Verified This Account Belongs to You." 
Now, did you ask them to verify whether the account belonged to
Bruce Cosma??? Absolutely not! This response to your letter is Totally
Irrelevant to what you requested and gave reason to. Your response back
to them should address this! 

Once you receive your responses from the credit bureaus, you can go
ahead and send off letter #2. This letter will be based on the response
your client receives. The information below is regarding a "Verified"
result response. Yes, you can include more than one item that remains on
the report in this letter. You could also choose to send each remaining
account in a separate letter. Your choice. 
Ways to reply to all other responses to your Round 1 letter can be found
in the previous lesson "Mastering Round 1."

A Round 2 Letter "MOV or Prove It w/ Factual Disputing" Might Sound
Like... 

(Credit Bureau), I am making a final goodwill attempt to have you clear
up this matter. The accounts you sent back verified are entirely
inaccurate, incomplete and represents a very serious error in your
reporting. Also be advised that this is my second time contacting you
company about this matter in writing. Your company is in violation of 15
U.S.C. § 1681. No need for me to go into detail and tell you the law.
Your company 

**KNOWS**for a fact that you are required under the FCRA to have
properly verified the accuracy of an account listed on my credit report
with physical documents.

As I am sure that you are well aware, current Federal case law states
that, 

**Consumer Reporting Agencies bear grave responsibilities to ensure the
accuracy of the accounts they report on and their responsibility must
consist of something more than merely parroting information received
from other sources.**That basically means that you can't just verify
my account by telling me it is verified. Nor can you verify my account
by telling me you contacted the creditor and the account I disputed is
"Verified". I demand you investigate these accounts using legal
procedures that ensure maximum accuracy. I Do Not want a "Parroting"
response based on your dispute codes and automated systems.

(Credit Bureau's Name), I request that you send me your method of
verification of the inaccurate accounts you claim to have verified. We
both know that if I requested you PROVE you actually conducted an
investigation as is required by law and by requesting that you send me
the documents you have in your files that you used to verify the
disputed accounts you would not be able to do so, which means that you
have NOT verified or you cannot verify any of these accounts and under
the FCRA - you are required to 

**"...promptly delete all information which cannot be verified."**

This law is as clear as daylight as to the Civil liability and the
remedy available to me for 

**"**negligent noncompliance" if you fail to comply with this Federal
Law. I am a litigious consumer and fully intend on pursuing litigation
in this matter to enforce my rights under the FCRA. I demand the
following accounts be verified or deleted immediately: (among other
ways, your reasons can be written any or all of the following ways...)

Account Name: ABC COMPANY Account Number: 12345XXXX
Reason: You failed to verify all data for this account, every notation,
dates and balances. This account needs to be removed IMMEDIATELY per the
FCRA.

Account Name: DEF COMPANY Account Number: 54321XXXX Reason: You failed
to verify all data for this account because this collection account is
reporting a past due amount. A collection account cannot be reporting a
past due amount, therefore, this account needs to be deleted IMMEDIATELY
per the FCRA.  

Account Name: GHI COMPANY Account Number: 13246XXXX Reason: The date of
last activity is inaccurate. Equifax is reporting xx/xx/xx, Experian is
reporting xx/xx/xx, and Transunion is reporting xx/xx/xx. There is only
one source of information, which is the original creditor of this
alleged account. How can the date of activity be different for every
bureau if all of you should be verifying your information from the same
source??? There is no possible way that you verified this account as you
have falsely claimed, IMMEDIATELY Delete this account per the FCRA. 
    

If your company,(Credit Bureau's Name), fails to comply with federal
regulations by credit reporting agencies, you will be required to appear
in a court venue 

**local to me**, in order to formally defend yourself. I will also have
your company investigated by the Federal Trade Commission. I am also
maintaining a careful record of my communications with you for the
purpose of filing a complaint with the FTC and the Attorney General's
office, should you continue in your willful non-compliance. 

Also, I would like an updated copy of my credit report mailed to the
address below once this investigation is complete. Address
Thank you,

Name (Typed or Written; Not Signed) 

Your Round 2 letter can be sent regular mail or certified. Your choice.
The benefit of certified is that it further strengthens your paper
trail. The law allows 15 days for the a response to the Method of
Verification Letter. 

During this waiting period:

*         Keep reminding your client to forward the bureau
replies to you as soon as they arrive. 

*         Remind your client to keep their credit monitoring
account active, so you can see the results of your work each month.

*         In between time, continue to provide value by helping
your client to understand their finances, simple budgeting, paying down
balances, to stop applying for credit, to keep old accounts open, to
never co-sign for anyone and to pay their bills on time.

